{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And the ark came to rest in the seventh month, on the seventeenth day of the month.</b> Rashi writes (Rashi on Genesis 8:4) — from here you learn that it was submerged eleven cubits in the water, according to the calculation written in his commentary. And this is indeed found in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 33:7). But since Rashi is precise in certain places following the midrashic aggadot and takes trouble to explain the plain sense of Scripture, he has given us license to do likewise, for there are seventy faces to the Torah, and the midrashim are many and divergent in the words of the Sages. And I say that this calculation they stated does not fit the language of the verse. For if we are to interpret \"the ark came to rest in the seventh month\" as referring to this same day mentioned when the rain ceased, and the waters receded from the earth, going and diminishing — not according to the count of the second month mentioned at the beginning of the pericope (Genesis 7:11) and according to the count stated at the end of the pericope (Genesis 8:14) — how is it possible that Scripture would immediately turn in the second verse and say \"until the tenth month\" according to a different count, so that the tenth month would refer to the cessation of the rains? And the proof from the ark that it was submerged in the water, because the diminution works out equally for all the days — one cubit per four days — is no proof, for it is well known regarding the diminishing of water that a great river, when it decreases one cubit per four days at its start, will decrease four cubits in a single day at its end. And so according to that calculation, the tops of the mountains were seen on the first of Av, and the waters dried up on the first of Tishrei — and behold, in sixty days they diminished by the entire height of the high mountains, which is several thousands of cubits. Moreover, he sent the dove on the seventeenth of Elul, and the waters were still over all the earth and the trees were submerged, and behold they all dried up in twelve days. And by way of reason, if it was submerged eleven cubits in the water, that being more than a third of its height, it would have sunk, because it was broad at the bottom and narrowing to one cubit, for it is the opposite of ships and has great heaviness. And what appears according to the plain sense is that the 150 days spoken of in the prevailing of the waters (Genesis 7:24) include the forty days of the rainfall, for in them was the principal increase and prevailing, and they began to diminish on the seventeenth of Nisan, and the ark came to rest after thirty days on the mountains of Ararat on the seventeenth of Iyyar, which is the seventh month from the rainfall, and after seventy-three days, on the first of Av, which is the tenth month from the rain, the tops of the mountains were seen. And so we have made a small correction in the language of the verse. But what seems correct in my eyes is that the 150 days were from the seventeenth day of the second month, which is Marcheshvan, until the seventeenth day of the seventh month, which is Nisan, and that is the day of the ark's resting. For then HaShem caused a strong east wind to pass all that night and made the waters go down, that they diminished greatly, and the ark came to rest. And the proof is that Scripture did not say here \"the waters diminished in such-and-such a month on such-and-such a day, and the waters went and diminished until the seventh month, and the ark came to rest, etc.\" as it said regarding the other diminishment when the tops of the mountains were seen — for on the day of the diminishment the ark came to rest. And the sequence regarding this matter is as follows: on the day the rain came down, the fountains of the deep burst forth and the windows of the heavens were opened, and the rain came down forty days, and during them the waters prevailed fifteen cubits above, and the rain ceased at the end of the forty days, but the fountains of the deep and the windows of the heavens remained open, and the air was very humid and the whole earth was full of water and it was not draining down the slopes and would never dry, and they stood thus in their strength until the completion of 150 days from the rainfall. And then HaShem caused a very strong wind in the heavens and on the earth, and the fountains of the deep were stopped up, for the waters flowing from them returned to their place until the deep was filled as it had been at the beginning and the openings of its springs were closed, and the windows of the heavens were closed, and the air dried greatly through the drying wind, and the waters that were on the earth — their moisture was licked up. And behold, the waters diminished greatly on that day, and the ark came to rest, for it had been submerged in the water two or three cubits, and after seventy-three days, on the first of the tenth month, which is the month of Tammuz, the tops of the mountains were seen, and at the end of forty days, on the first of the eleventh month, on the tenth of the month, Noah opened the window of the ark, and after three weeks the dove went forth from him, and after thirty days he removed the covering of the ark." "And the meaning of <b>and Elohim caused a wind to pass over the earth</b> is that there was a great and strong wind coming forth from the bowels of the earth over the face of the deep and hovering over the waters, and through it the fountains of the deep were stopped up, for Scripture did not say \"and Elohim caused a wind to pass over the waters\":" "<b>And the waters abated.</b> That the waters flowing from the deep came to rest — from the language of \"the king's wrath has abated\" (Esther 7:10), which means subsided. Or it is an expression of hiding a thing and swallowing it up, to teach that the waters of the deep were swallowed up in their place. And so he said in Seder Olam (4): the waters that went upward — the wind dried them up, and those that went downward were swallowed up in their place." "And the meaning of <b>and the rain was restrained from the heavens</b> is that no more rain fell until they went out from the ark, for the heavens were like iron through this wind, and they did not bring down dew or rain at all, and the air dried and the waters vanished, for the rain of the Flood had already ended from the fortieth day." "<b>And the waters receded from the earth, going and returning.</b> He says that they diminished little by little until the surface of the ground dried up. And \"the waters diminished at the end of a hundred and fifty days\" is attached to \"the ark came to rest,\" to say that on that day the waters diminished by a great diminishment through which the ark came to rest, as I have explained."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The repeated phrase וַיַּחְסְרוּ הַמַּיִם מִקְצֵה in the second proposed reading may be textually unstable; translated hyper-literally as it stands.", 2 "The pronoun \"he\" in \"so he said in Seder Olam\" refers to the author of that work, traditionally attributed to Rabbi Yose ben Halafta."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:26:37.121782542Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}